uniform_against	one
# "Uniform experience against x" could be read two ways. One reading is the experience that established a law x would break (S1/P2). The other is that x has never been observed in any age or country (S5, S6, A1).
# The text treats these as the same thing: a law is established by exceptionless experience, so anything that breaks it has never been observed. I judged this not a change of sense.
# The familiar worry about whether "uniform" covers all testimony or only our own experience is a question of whether the argument assumes its conclusion. No statement here takes the word one way and another statement the other way.
proof_against	one
